olyto/api-php` image. Define it once near the top of `setup.sh` as a variable (default `33`, overridable via env such as `SOLYTO_UID`/`SOLYTO_GID`), and add a one-line comment explaining why it is numeric.\n  files: setup.sh\n  depends: none\n  risk: low — same effect on Debian/Ubuntu hosts (www-data is already 33 there), and it removes the dependency on a host user; the only risk is if the image is not actually UID 33, so confirm that first (see Context).\n\nTASK-2 (needs human confirmation before implementing): Make the ownership step not abort the script when it isn't running as root. If `$EUID` ≠ 0, use `sudo chown` if `sudo` is available. Otherwise print a clear warning with the exact manual command (`sudo chown -R 33:33 solyto/storage`) and continue.\n  files: setup.sh\n  depends: TASK-1\n  risk: medium — it changes failure behaviour (warn vs. abort), and with `curl | bash` a `sudo` password prompt has to read from `/dev/tty`, as the existing `read` calls already do.\n\nTASK-3: Add a short note to the README install section saying that `storage/` is owned by UID/GID 33 (www-data inside the containers), that the script may need root/sudo for this, and how to override or fix it manually.\n  files: README.md\n  depends: TASK-1 (and TASK-2 if it is approved)\n  risk: low — documentation only.\n\n## Open questions / unclear scope (not turned into tasks)\n\n1. **Other non-portable parts of setup.sh.** \"All OSes\" may mean more than\n   the chown. `sed -i -e …` (setup.sh:20-25) is GNU-only. On macOS/BSD,\n   `-i` takes the next argument as a backup suffix, so it will misbehave or\n   leave a `.env-e` file. If macOS hosts are in scope, a follow-up task could\n   swap it for a portable form (e.g. `sed -i.bak … && rm .env.bak`). I left\n   it out because the brief only describes the chown failure.\n2. **Detecting the UID dynamically** (e.g.\n   `docker run --rm --entrypoint id solyto/api-php:$SOLYTO_VERSION -u www-data`)\n   would always be correct, but it pulls the image during setup and needs\n   Docker access in the script. I suggest hardcoding with an env override\n   (TASK-1) unless the image's UID is expected to change.\n3. **Base image confirmation.** Someone with Docker access should confirm\n   that `www-data` in `solyto/api-php` is 33:33 before TASK-1 is merged.\n# Implementation: Fix setup.sh for all OSes\n\nid: plus\ndeveloper: @developer\ndate: 2026-10-14\n\n## Summary\n\n`setup.sh` no longer looks up the user name `www-data` on the host. It now\n`chown`s `storage/` to a numeric UID/GID (default 33:33, which is www-data\ninside the `solyto/api-php` containers). You can override it with `SOLYTO_UID` /\n`SOLYTO_GID`. The README explains the ownership step and how to fix it by hand.\nTASK-2 was **not** implemented because tasks.md marks it as needing human\nconfirmation, and this session ran unattended.\n\n## Changes\n\nTASK-1: `setup.sh` — added `SOLYTO_UID`/`SOLYTO_GID` (default 33, can be overridden from the\nenv) near the top, with a comment on why the IDs are numeric. Replaced\n`chown -R www-data:www-data storage/` with `chown -R \"${SOLYTO_UID}:${SOLYTO_GID}\" storage/`.\nChecked with `bash -n`.\n\nTASK-2: skipped (needs human confirmation). It is about warning or using sudo\ninstead of stopping when not running as root.\n\nTASK-3: `README.md` — added a note under \"Install script\": storage/ is owned by 33:33\n(container www-data), you need root/`sudo bash`, how to use `SOLYTO_UID`/`SOLYTO_GID`,\nand the manual `sudo chown -R 33:33 solyto/storage` fix. The wording matches the\ncurrent behaviour (the script stops if chown fails). Update it if TASK-2 is approved.\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\n- Not yet confirmed that `www-data` in `solyto/api-php` is UID/GID 33. Docker was not\n  available in this session. Please run\n  `docker run --rm --entrypoint id solyto/api-php:latest www-data` before merging.\n  (The Arch fix with 33:33 suggests it is.)\n- TASK-2 is waiting for a decision.\n- `sed -i -e …` in setup.sh only works with GNU sed and will break on macOS/BSD.\n  This is open question 1 in tasks.md and was not in scope.\n- The working tree shows `/workspace/.env` as modified, but git cannot hash it\n  (\"unsupported file type\", probably a mount). It was not touched or committed.\ncat: /workspace/.manigot/manigot.json: No such file or directory","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LZUdKLnkmm4g9V8VWhio9a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
